3.46.m:
Mārkaṇḍeya said:
Formerly, in the Kṛta age, the people were established in knowledge, O king;
because people were established in knowledge then, O scion of the Yadu race,

3.230
The Swan said:
[The food] of cultivators, imprisoned thieves, eunuchs, actors, / fallen persons, outcasts, villains, and prison guards;
of menstruating women, childless widows, heretics, executioners, / physicians, hunters, unchaste women, enemies of the king;
of leather-workers, professional warriors, weapon sellers, / washermen, cruel men, the afflicted, those ruled by women, communal priests;
of tailors, usurers, forgers, and those who sacrifice for śūdras— / the food of these must not be eaten, nor of anyone who offers it without respect.
Food distributed by turn, sneezed upon, proclaimed aloud, looked at by an outcaste, / given as a fee, stale, leftover, as well as [the food] of one who lives on leftovers;
and that touched by a menstruating woman, that smelled by a cow or a dog, / fallen upon by hair or insects, and likewise eaten of by a bird;
the food of a slave, a sharecropper, a cowherd, and a barber—though not of others— / as well as the food of a śūdra, ancestral offerings, and [the food] of one who sells himself.
Stale food may be eaten if smeared with fat, like seasoned dishes, / and even without fat, wheat, barley, matured grains, and nimba fruits.

Except for that of goats, cows, and buffaloes, all milks [are forbidden], / and even theirs within ten days of calving, and that of a cow whose calf has died.
The kapiñjala partridge, the tittiri partridge, the peacock, and the quail— / these birds are indeed edible, but never the rest.
The hare, the porcupine, the monitor lizard, the rhinoceros, and likewise the turtle: / they declare these edible among five-toed animals, but those with teeth in both jaws are inedible.
All single-hoofed animals must be avoided, as well as known animals and birds [not prescribed], / and garlic, carrot, onion, and mushrooms;
and red tree-resins, the śigru, and the śleṣmātaka; / and pieces of hooves are likewise inedible for the twice-born;
